Ladybird rejects a bytecode cache blob unless it carries the fingerprint
of the running build’s instruction set — a hash of the instruction types
build.rs generates from interpreter.flap — besides the hand-bumped
FORMAT_VERSION, the runtime, the program type, and the source hash. So
it keeps a warm cache across rebuilds that leave the instructions alone,
and drops it whenever an opcode or an operand changes.

The other engines tie their caches to the whole build instead. V8’s
SerializedCodeData::SanityCheck() rejects a code cache entry whose
Version::Hash() or FlagList::Hash() differs from the running V8’s.
JavaScriptCore stores computeJSCBytecodeCacheVersion(), a hash of the
UUID of the JavaScriptCore binary, and its isUpToDate() rejects any
other. And SpiderMonkey fails a stencil transcoded under another build
ID than JS::GetScriptTranscodingBuildId() gives with Failure_BadBuildId.
